{"no_themes_found":false,"themes":[{"theme_id":"T1","label":"App-facilitated invisibility reinforces social constraints","description":"While delivery apps offer a temporary escape from social judgment by allowing private choices, they ultimately prevent people from challenging social pressures in real life, reinforcing their dependence on these digital workarounds.","descriptive_prominence":"HIGH","quotes":[{"turn_id":"T034","speaker":"P1","quote":"But it solves it by removing you from the picture entirely, which is part of what's killing off the actual spaces where you wouldn't need to solve it in the first place."},{"turn_id":"T041","speaker":"P2","quote":"The app doesn't liberate you from the performance. It just makes the performance invisible so you stop noticing you're doing it."}]},{"theme_id":"T2","label":"Spatial and cultural variation in pressure","description":"The ability to challenge social norms varies significantly depending on geography, with fragmented urban areas offering diverse, less judgmental spaces compared to close-knit communities with rigid, inescapable expectations.","descriptive_prominence":"MEDIUM","quotes":[{"turn_id":"T036","speaker":"P1","quote":"It's fragmented, like Amir was saying about Birmingham. There are different spaces with different vibes. So it's not about you individually deciding to be uncomfortable and see if you can push through it."},{"turn_id":"T042","speaker":"P3","quote":"Because I'm not in a fragmented space where different things are normal in different areas. I'm in a place where I know people, where the culture is solid, where ordering something that reads as soft would actually get clocked."}]},{"theme_id":"T3","label":"Real social costs of defying norms","description":"For some participants, defying peer group expectations is not a simple matter of choosing to be brave, but carries tangible and long-lasting social costs within their communities.","descriptive_prominence":"MEDIUM","quotes":[{"turn_id":"T042","speaker":"P3","quote":"It's staying strong because testing it would actually cost me something. And like, you could say well, maybe it's worth it to test it, maybe things would shift if you did. But that's easy to say when you're not the one living in the place where it would shift."},{"turn_id":"T043","speaker":"P2","quote":"It's that testing it would actually break something real in your life, so the app isn't a workaround for a problem you could solve if you were braver."}]},{"theme_id":"T4","label":"Internalized pressure and self-imposed invisibility","description":"Participants describe how social pressure becomes internalized as a subconscious reflex, leading them to self-censor and choose invisibility even when direct, external judgment may not actually occur.","descriptive_prominence":"MEDIUM","quotes":[{"turn_id":"T040","speaker":"P3","quote":"Even though we're not really mates, even though they're probably not thinking about me at all — I'm still kind of... accounting for them, you know?"},{"turn_id":"T045","speaker":"P5","quote":"But I also think I'm kind of... I'm accepting it as more solid than it actually is? Like, I'm not Will — I don't have the same actual social consequences if I do something different. But I'm acting like I do."}]
      }
    ]}

{
  "no_themes_found": false,
  "themes": [
    {
      "theme_id": "T1",
      "label": "Defaulting to familiar routines",
      "description": "Participants describe a strong tendency to stick with familiar food and shopping routines because doing so is easier and requires less mental effort than making new decisions.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "HIGH",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T013",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "I default just as much as anyone — maybe more, honestly. Like, I know where the good takeaways are, I know what I usually get, and unless I'm making a real effort I'll just end up in the same rotation."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T014",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "Even when I'm thinking I should try something different, I don't really. It's just easier to stick with what works."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T016",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "Whether it's because there's literally nothing else or because trying something new requires more effort than just getting what you know — the end result's pretty similar. You're not choosing, you're just doing what's easiest."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "theme_id": "T2",
      "label": "Geographic constraints versus personal choice",
      "description": "Speakers compare how a literal lack of physical options in rural areas contrasts with the self-imposed, convenience-based restrictions of urban residents who have many options.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "HIGH",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T012",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "It's just... logistics are a bit different when you're not in a city with loads of options, you know?"
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T015",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "Whereas for me, I'm locked into the same rotation even though there are other options I could try if I actually bothered. It's almost worse in a way, because I can't even blame it on lack of options."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T016",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "Because yeah, there's only one shop out here, but it's not like I'm sat at home thinking \"I could drive twenty minutes to the next town if I really wanted to.\" That's just not realistic for a weeknight when you're knackered after work."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "no_themes_found": false,
  "themes": [
    {
      "theme_id": "T1",
      "label": "Navigating social costs and banter",
      "description": "Participants discuss how making different food choices around male friends involves accepting social consequences like banter or teasing rather than simply conforming or pretending not to care.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "HIGH",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T029",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "Sometimes you just... prefer a burger. And sometimes you pick it because you know your mates will take the piss, and that's just part of being mates, innit? That's not the same as being trapped or conforming without knowing."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T030",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "Not because I couldn't physically do it — obviously you can eat plant-based — but because the social side of it would be too much hassle. You'd get constant banter, and yeah, maybe you'd be fine with that eventually, but it'd be an effort you'd have to be willing to take on."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T031",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "You don't have to not care about what they think — you can care, but also just decide that's what you're eating anyway. You take the jokes, you deal with it, but you're doing it because you actually want to."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T032",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "It's just you deciding that what you want to do matters more than avoiding the jokes. That's actually a real choice in a way I wasn't really thinking about."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "theme_id": "T2",
      "label": "Structural and practical barriers to eating plant-based",
      "description": "Participants highlight that adopting a plant-based diet involves significant structural obstacles, such as lack of local dining options and the necessity of increased planning and home cooking.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "HIGH",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T033",
          "speaker": "P3",
          "quote": "But the thing is, if I actually wanted to go plant-based, the bigger issue would just be... practicality. Like, where do you even get that stuff out here? There's not loads of options."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T034",
          "speaker": "P4",
          "quote": "Because what you're describing — the practicality stuff, having to cook more, limited options when you're out — that's not the same as not caring enough. That's actually... you do care, but the actual barriers are real, you know?"
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T035",
          "speaker": "P3",
          "quote": "But the structural stuff — the places that don't have options, the distance, the planning — I can't really change that by just deciding to care more. It's just how it is out here."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "no_themes_found": false,
  "themes": [
    {
      "theme_id": "T1",
      "label": "Motivation Needed Beyond Simple Infrastructure",
      "description": "Having the physical infrastructure and options to choose plant-based food is meaningless without an underlying personal reason or motivation to care about making that choice.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "MEDIUM",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T037",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "So removing the barriers might make it theoretically possible, but it wouldn't necessarily make it appealing."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T038",
          "speaker": "P3",
          "quote": "David's right — you'd need to actually care about it for a reason, not just because the option exists."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T039",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "And without caring about it, the infrastructure doesn't matter because you've got no reason to use it anyway."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "theme_id": "T2",
      "label": "Invisibility of Choices Outside One's Environment",
      "description": "When a choice is completely absent from a person's immediate social circle and physical environment, it becomes invisible, making it impossible to actively choose or reject.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "HIGH",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T040",
          "speaker": "P3",
          "quote": "The infrastructure isn't there, so it's harder to do. But also because it's harder and because nobody's doing it, I'm not really... imagining it as something I could do."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T041",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "plant-based isn't there, so it's not even a possibility to think about or choose or not choose. It's just absent."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T042",
          "speaker": "P4",
          "quote": "It's not about whether you care enough or whether you can handle the banter. It's that whole categories of things — ways of living, things you could do — they're just not even visible to you."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "theme_id": "T3",
      "label": "Identity Expectations Constraining Personal Choices",
      "description": "Regional and masculine identity expectations shape and narrow down what options feel permissible or possible for an individual before they even make conscious decisions.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "HIGH",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T043",
          "speaker": "P3",
          "quote": "Not because anyone's saying you can't do them, but because they don't really fit into what being a man in a rural area looks like."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T044",
          "speaker": "P4",
          "quote": "before you even get to that point — before the question of whether you're willing to take the stick — you're already narrowed down what you'd even want to do based on what feels like it's for someone like you."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T043",
          "speaker": "P3",
          "quote": "You're only being intentional within a certain narrow set of options that feel acceptable for who you're supposed to be."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "no_themes_found": false,
  "themes": [
    {
      "theme_id": "T1",
      "label": "Gender-coded food and group conformity",
      "description": "Men experience social pressure to order 'safe,' masculine-coded foods like burgers or pies to conform in group settings, while avoiding choices perceived as health-conscious or feminine.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "HIGH",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T017",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "Like, you're not ordering a salad or something that might look... I dunno, too careful or whatever. You just get the burger or the pie or the chips. It's not a conscious thing most of the time, it's just what you do."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T018",
          "speaker": "P3",
          "quote": "you know what's acceptable to order with mates and what isn't."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T024",
          "speaker": "P4",
          "quote": "a big green salad with like, dressing on it, that feels coded as a bit feminine or like you're being careful or whatever."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "theme_id": "T2",
      "label": "Contextual influences on social pressure",
      "description": "The pressure to choose conforming foods varies depending on social context, with men feeling freer to order what they want when eating alone, with close friends, or in anonymous settings compared to unfamiliar or small-community settings.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "HIGH",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T017",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "But then when I'm on my own at the curry house, I can get whatever — rice, veg, whatever — and no one's watching it, so it doesn't matter."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T020",
          "speaker": "P3",
          "quote": "No one's judging because we all know each other properly. But I reckon if you put me somewhere unfamiliar with new faces, I'd probably slip back into that a bit, without thinking."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T022",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "Like, where I live there's not loads of places, so you see the same people. You go to the same chippy, the same couple of pubs. You're not anonymous."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "theme_id": "T3",
      "label": "Logical awareness versus behavioral change",
      "description": "Recognizing that others do not care about one's food choices does not necessarily eliminate the underlying social awareness or make it easy to overcome the default preference for fitting in.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "MEDIUM",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T021",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "It's easier to just go for the safe burger like everyone else and not have it be a thing."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T022",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "But there's still that moment when you're looking at the menu and you think about it, you know? It's not like knowing it doesn't matter makes the awareness go away."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T023",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "The feeling's still there even when you know it shouldn't be."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "no_themes_found": false,
  "themes": [
    {
      "theme_id": "T1",
      "label": "Household and partner dietary dynamics",
      "description": "Speakers discuss how their home eating patterns are closely tied to their partners' cooking, meaning a shift to a plant-based diet would require difficult domestic negotiations or preparing separate meals.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "MEDIUM",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T026",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "I mean, my wife does most of the cooking at home, so it wouldn't just be me deciding that, would it? That'd be a conversation with her."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T026",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "If I actually went plant-based I'd probably have to cook a lot more of my own stuff, because I'm not going to ask her to completely change how she cooks for the whole family."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T028",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "I'm not saying she'd shut it down completely or anything. I just... I don't know, it's not something that's on the table for us, really."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "theme_id": "T2",
      "label": "Social visibility of a plant-based diet",
      "description": "Participants distinguish between ordering a single meatless dish and adopting a plant-based diet, noting that the latter represents an ongoing, highly visible lifestyle choice that labels them socially.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "HIGH",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T031",
          "speaker": "P3",
          "quote": "And at work, if you're getting lunch with people, it becomes this thing. You become the plant-based guy."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T032",
          "speaker": "P3",
          "quote": "But going plant-based is different, isn't it? That's not just one meal, that's a whole... change. And then every time you're out, every time you're ordering something, it's this visible thing."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T033",
          "speaker": "P4",
          "quote": "But if you're doing it all the time, then you're the plant-based guy. That's different."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "theme_id": "T3",
      "label": "Constraints versus lack of interest",
      "description": "Speakers differentiate between genuine external obstacles holding them back from dietary shifts and a basic absence of interest, where practical difficulties only matter if there is a desire to change.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "HIGH",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T029",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "And the thing is, if you're not actually wanting to do it in the first place, then yeah \u2014 all that extra stuff, the conversations, people clocking it, it's just not worth the hassle for something you're not bothered about anyway."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T030",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "I'm not secretly wanting to go plant-based and making excuses about it. I'm just not interested in it. So all those constraints \u2014 the wife, the options round here, what people would think \u2014 they're not really constraints then, are they?"
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T030",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "For me it's more just that the question doesn't really apply. I'm not wanting to change what I eat."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "theme_id": "T4",
      "label": "Geographic location shaping food choices",
      "description": "Participants contrast living in metropolitan areas like London, where diverse options allow food choices to blend in, with smaller suburbs or towns where limited choices make alternative diets highly noticeable.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "MEDIUM",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T025",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "I think maybe it's different where you lot are with more options and more people around, you know?"
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T028",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "Where I am, if I started going to the chippy ordering chips and beans or whatever because I'd gone plant-based, people would notice. It's just different."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T029",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "I suppose where I am it might feel different because there's so much choice and so many people doing different things that it's easier to just... do your thing without it being this whole statement about it."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "no_themes_found": false,
  "themes": [
    {
      "theme_id": "T1",
      "label": "The ongoing friction and cost of lifestyle change",
      "description": "Choosing a plant-based diet is seen as an ongoing, daily effort that introduces constant friction into social interactions and food choices, rather than a simple one-time decision.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "MEDIUM",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T031",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "But going plant-based when everyone around you isn't? That's not just a choice you make once. That's you having to actively manage it every time you eat, every time you're out with people. You're not just trying something — you're sticking to something that everyone else isn't doing."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T031",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "It's more that once you actually understand what it would cost — the effort, the social stuff — you're not sure you want to do it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "theme_id": "T2",
      "label": "Plant-based food as a culinary compromise",
      "description": "There is a strong perception that plant-based alternatives are inherently less satisfying, feeling like an unappealing health compromise rather than proper food.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "MEDIUM",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T032",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "It's that plant-based stuff, for a lot of blokes like us, just doesn't feel like proper food, you know?"
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T032",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "going plant-based isn't about choosing something better. It's about choosing something that's supposed to be better for you, but tastes like less."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "theme_id": "T3",
      "label": "Constructing rationalizations for inertia",
      "description": "Participants self-reflectively realize that their list of barriers and complaints about plant-based food are post-hoc rationalizations to justify their unwillingness to step out of their comfort zone, rather than obstacles based on actual experience.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "HIGH",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T033",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "I haven't really tested any of it. I've just... settled into what I think and haven't questioned it."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T034",
          "speaker": "P3",
          "quote": "I've been listing barriers like they're facts, but they're more like... things I think would be barriers because I haven't bothered to find out."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T035",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "I think I might be listing them because I've already decided I'm not going to bother, and I need it to sound like a rational choice rather than just... not bothering."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T035",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "I'm avoiding it because it's easier not to think about it, and then I'm making up reasons afterwards to make it sound deliberate."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "no_themes_found": false,
  "themes": [
    {
      "theme_id": "T1",
      "label": "Influence of weekday habits on defaults",
      "description": "Participants discuss how casual, unthinking eating choices on weekday nights can feed into broader eating habits and shape general food defaults.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "MEDIUM",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T018",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "the habits you build on those Tuesday nights kind of feed into everything else, you know?"
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T019",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "you build these lazy defaults and then you're stuck with them."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "theme_id": "T2",
      "label": "Active choice to avoid food origins",
      "description": "Some participants describe making a conscious choice to remain ignorant about where their meat comes from in order to protect their comfort with eating it.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "MEDIUM",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T022",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "There's stuff I could know, stuff I could think about, but I actively choose not to because it would change how I feel about eating it."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T023",
          "speaker": "P3",
          "quote": "But I get what Ibrahim's saying about not wanting to go there."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "theme_id": "T3",
      "label": "Geographic context and local defaults",
      "description": "The ease or difficulty of making conscious food choices is viewed as highly dependent on geography, with city environments presenting more friction to connecting with food sources than rural areas.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "MEDIUM",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T024",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "where I am, in Newcastle, that's not really an option in the same way, you know? I'm not gonna build relationships with farmers the way you have."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T025",
