    {
      "theme_id": "T4",
      "label": "Gendered social policing of food choices",
      "description": "Participants explore how male peer groups police food choices through 'banter' and social pressure, framing certain foods like meat as masculine and others like salad as non-masculine.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "HIGH",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T026",
          "speaker": "P4",
          "quote": "if I'm out with my mates and one of them orders a salad, you're gonna get a bit of banter about it."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T027",
          "speaker": "P3",
          "quote": "I reckon some of that banter is kind of self-policing, you know? Like, you're preemptively taking the piss because you think you're supposed to, not because anyone's actually forcing you."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T028",
          "speaker": "P5",
          "quote": "if you're actually the person ordering the salad in a group where nobody else is doing that, it doesn't feel like just a neutral choice, does it? It feels like you're making a statement or something."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "no_themes_found": false,
  "themes": [
    {
      "theme_id": "T1",
      "label": "Social friction of dietary change",
      "description": "For many speakers, the primary barrier to going plant-based is the social difficulty of explaining their choice, facing questions, or distancing themselves from community relationships.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "MEDIUM",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T030",
          "speaker": "P1",
          "quote": "But I think what would actually be hard is the social side of it, weirdly — given what we've just been talking about. Because for me, it's not about the banter thing, it's more about... the relationships I've built, you know?"
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T034",
          "speaker": "P3",
          "quote": "where I'd struggle — and I'm being honest here — is more the social side of it in Glasgow. Not even the banter necessarily, just... it would feel a bit weird. People would notice, they'd ask questions, and I'd have to explain myself."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T035",
          "speaker": "P4",
          "quote": "the thing that would actually stop me... yeah, it's the social side of it, like David said. Not even the banter so much, just... it would be weird. People would clock it, ask questions, and I'd have to have some reason to justify it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "theme_id": "T2",
      "label": "Comfort of the default diet",
      "description": "Staying with a non-plant-based diet is driven by a personal enjoyment of meat and dairy combined with a lack of compelling motivation or urgency to change.",
      "descriptive_prominence": "HIGH",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "turn_id": "T031",
          "speaker": "P2",
          "quote": "I eat meat because that's what I eat. It's what I'm used to, it's what feels normal, and I actually like it."
        },
        {
          "turn_id": "T034",
          "speaker": "P3",
          "quote": "I eat meat, I like it fine, it's normal. But I don't have this strong attachment to it the way some people seem to. Like, I could probably go plant-based if I had to."
        },
        {
